            "target_entity_instance": "average slope as geometric factor",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "The only geometric factor included in the analytical glacier model is the average slope, while assuming a constant glacier width.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Temperature reconstruction from glacier length fluctuations (Leclercq and Oerlemans, 2011)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "global mean temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Our reconstruction [The Temperature reconstruction from glacier length fluctuations (Leclercq and Oerlemans, 2011)] shows a fairly constant global mean temperature for the period 1600\u20131830. From 1830 until 1940, temperatures continuously increased by 0.61 \u00b1 0.26 K. From 1940 to 1970, there is a temporal decrease of the global mean temperature of 0.07 \u00b1 0.12 K.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Temperature reconstruction from glacier length fluctuations (Leclercq and Oerlemans, 2011)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "NH temperature anomalies",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The reconstructed NH temperature anomalies (Fig. 4b) show results that are similar to the global reconstruction. The glacier reconstruction shows constant temperatures for the period 1600\u20131700, followed by a gradual cooling from 1700 to 1850.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Temperature reconstruction from glacier length fluctuations (Leclercq and Oerlemans, 2011)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "SH temperature anomalies",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The reconstruction of SH temperature anomalies is shown in Fig. 4c. The reconstruction gives a SH temperature increase of 1.27 \u00b1 0.76 for the period 1850\u20132000.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Temperature reconstruction from glacier length fluctuations (Leclercq and Oerlemans, 2011)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "European annual temperature anomalies",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The reconstruction of European annual temperature anomalies from glacier length is in far better agreement with the instrumental temperature record than the reconstructions of the individual regions (Fig. 5).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
